+/*
+ * WHEN THE PICTURE IS ALWAYS THE REASON, MAKE A SMALLER PICTURE.
+ *
+ * The skip below is the answer to ONE frame the rasterizer made expensive:
+ * show the last picture again and the game keeps its sixty. It is the wrong
+ * answer to a whole SCENE the rasterizer cannot afford, because then it
+ * fires over and over, and a picture repeated every few frames for thirty
+ * seconds is the judder a player calls stutter. Measured on an RG556 at
+ * --hd3d 3: the field holds sixty with half the frame spare, and the title
+ * demo, one fill-heavy 3D scene, skips 14.6% of its pictures.
+ *
+ * The port already knows the right trade there and prints it as advice:
+ * step down a scale, because the 2D art is the same picture at every
+ * setting and only the 3D layer's sharpness changes. This takes the advice
+ * automatically, for as long as the scene needs it, instead of asking the
+ * player to take it for the whole session.
+ *
+ * A RATE, NOT A RUN, and counting consecutive frames was the first version
+ * and did not fire once. A frame the picture made late is followed by a
+ * frame with room, every time, because the pacer's own rule for a late
+ * frame is to start the next one immediately, so it gets a whole interval
+ * to do one frame's work in and lands early. Consecutive misses therefore
+ * never reach two even in a scene missing half its frames. What is wanted
+ * is how OFTEN the picture is the reason, so this leaks: four for a miss,
+ * one back for a frame with room, and a drop when the balance says the
+ * picture is behind more than about a fifth of them. In the scene that
+ * needs it that is a quarter of a second; at a twentieth of that rate it
+ * never arrives.
+ *
+ * Asymmetric on purpose. Down in a quarter of a second, so a scene that
+ * turns heavy costs a few pictures and not a hundred; up only after four
+ * clean seconds, so a scene sitting near the line does not oscillate
+ * between two sharpnesses, which would be more visible than either. Never
+ * above what the run asked for, and never in a run that asked for native,
+ * at scale 1 this is not compiled out, it simply never does anything, which
+ * keeps every byte-exact gate looking at the same code it always did.
+ *
+ * It lives inside the pacer, so an unpaced run, a benchmark, a replay
+ * being diffed, the selftests, never reaches it and renders at the fixed
+ * scale it was given.
+ */
+/*
+ * EVERY MISS WEIGHS THE SAME, and weighting it by the overrun was tried and
+ * is worse. It is the more obvious design, a frame twenty milliseconds
+ * over is worse than one two over, and it does what it promises, dropping
+ * in three frames instead of six. But arriving sooner also means recovering
+ * sooner, into a scene that has not finished being heavy: on the RG556's
+ * title demo it turned three scale changes in seventy-five seconds into
+ * eight, and a picture whose sharpness pumps up and down four times a scene
+ * is more noticeable than one that is softer throughout.
+ *
+ * The repeated-present counts came out slightly worse too, but not by
+ * enough to say so: two runs of THIS policy on the same scene read 1.31%
+ * and 1.68%, so anything inside half a point of that is the device, not the
+ * design. The change count is the part that is not noise, and it is the
+ * part a player sees. A flat weight keeps the controller slow enough to be
+ * boring, which is the whole job.
+ */
+#define VIEW_HD_MISS_W   4u             /* a picture-caused miss weighs this */
+#define VIEW_HD_DROP_AT  24u            /* ...and this much of it drops a scale */
+#define VIEW_HD_CLEAR_N  240u           /* four clean seconds put one back */
+
+/*
+ * DID THIS FRAME COST A PICTURE? One question, asked in one place, because
+ * the two things that act on the answer have to agree about it: the skip
+ * below, which drops the next picture, and the scale loop above, which
+ * decides the scene needs a smaller one. When they disagreed, one of them
+ * was always wrong about the same frame.
+ *
+ * A frame costs a picture when it is past its deadline AND both of these
+ * hold:
+ *
+ *   the picture is a real part of why, a quarter of the overrun or more.
+ *   Below that the frame was made expensive by the GAME, not the rasterizer:
+ *   a map load runs 100 ms with 1 ms of rasterize behind it, and dropping
+ *   three pictures there saves four milliseconds of a hundred while the
+ *   player, whose game has just stopped dead, watches it stay stopped three
+ *   frames longer.
+ *
+ *   the overrun is worth half a picture. THIS IS THE HALF THAT WAS MISSING,
+ *   and it cost the owner most of their pictures for days. The first test
+ *   asks whether the picture is big enough to have caused the overrun; it
+ *   never asked whether the overrun is big enough to be worth a picture, and
+ *   those come apart exactly where it matters, a frame a tenth of a
+ *   millisecond late with fifteen milliseconds of rasterize behind it passes
+ *   the first test easily, and a whole picture is spent to recover a tenth
+ *   of a millisecond.
+ *
+ * It does not stop at one, because a skipped frame is cheap: the next frame
+ * finds room, draws in full, goes a tenth of a millisecond over again, and
+ * skips again. The run settles into drawing every other picture and stays
+ * there. Measured in the owner's own Windows log (r1169 at --hd3d 3,
+ * 2026-09-07): thirteen consecutive five-second windows reading `150 of 300
+ * frames late, 150 pictures skipped`, the late frames averaging 16.78 ms of
+ * work against a 16.67 ms budget. A machine seven parts in a thousand short
+ * of the frame was giving up half of every picture it drew, and the advice
+ * line concluded that it `cannot draw every frame at --hd3d 3`. Reproduced
+ * here headless on the Jubilife station at --hd3d 2, 684 columns, one render
+ * thread: five straight windows of exactly 150 of 300, and 225 of 300 at
+ * --hd3d 3, which is the three-in-a-row cap drawing one picture in four.
+ *
+ * WHY HALF A PICTURE AND NOT A WHOLE ONE. A skip saves exactly picture_ns,
+ * the rasterize and the publish it stands in front of, which is what that
+ * variable is measured as, so a whole one is the level at which a skip
+ * pays the debt off and no more. But the threshold does NOT set how often a
+ * skip happens: that is fixed by how far behind the machine actually falls
+ * per frame, one skip per (picture_ns / that), which is the fewest that can
+ * hold sixty and is the same at any threshold. What the threshold sets is
+ * how much debt is CARRIED between skips, and carried debt is input latency
+ * and reads as `slip` in a trace, where a large one has already been
+ * misdiagnosed once as the Windows timer. Half a picture halves both and
+ * costs nothing.
+ */
+static int view_picture_is_the_bill(long long ahead,
+                                    unsigned long long picture_ns)
+{
+    unsigned long long debt;
+
+    if (ahead >= 0) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    debt = (unsigned long long)(-ahead);
+    return picture_ns * 4ull >= debt && debt * 2ull >= picture_ns;
+}
+
+/*
+ * WOULD THE SCALE ABOVE STILL FIT? ASK BEFORE GOING BACK UP TO IT.
+ *
+ * "Four clean seconds put one back" is a rule about the scale being drawn
+ * NOW, and that is not the question being asked. A scene has room at 1x
+ * precisely BECAUSE it is at 1x, and the scene likeliest to have room at 1x
+ * is the one just dropped out of 2x for missing its frames. So the counter
+ * fills, the scale goes up, the misses start again, six of them put it back
+ * down, and the loop's two halves spend the session undoing each other.
+ *
+ * Measured here on the Jubilife station at --hd3d 4, 684 columns, one render
+ * thread, over two minutes: twenty drops against seventeen recoveries,
+ * settling on nothing. A player watching that sees the 3D layer's sharpness
+ * pump up and down all the way through a scene, which is what the counters'
+ * asymmetry (down in a quarter of a second, up after four clean ones) was
+ * chosen to prevent. It cannot: the asymmetry sets that oscillation's PERIOD
+ * and nothing in the loop tests the thing that decides whether it happens.
+ *
+ * WHAT THE SCALE ABOVE WOULD COST, since the scale is a multiplier on each
+ * axis: at s+1 the rasterizer covers ((s+1)/s)^2 of the pixels it covers at
+ * s, so the picture costs that much more, ((2s+1)/s^2) MORE, in the form
+ * this needs. Cost does not follow pixel count exactly, because setup, the
+ * edge walk and the per-polygon work go as the first power or as none;
+ * measured on this station with the pacer OFF, so that no skipped frame
+ * dilutes the mean, the 3D span went 11.42 ms a frame at 2x to 22.02 at 3x,
+ * which is 1.93x where the pixels alone say 2.25x. The estimate is therefore
+ * about a sixth high, and high is the safe direction here: it is a margin,
+ * in a loop that has just been shown to move too readily.
+ *
+ * Estimating from the CURRENT picture rather than remembering what the scale
+ * above cost last time is deliberate. A remembered cost is the cost of the
+ * scene that failed, and by the time the loop wants to go back up the player
+ * has usually walked somewhere else, so one heavy town would hold the
+ * picture soft for the whole of the light cave after it.
+ *
+ * Both of this scale's terms are taken over the WHOLE clean stretch and not
+ * off its last frame, the worst picture in it and the least slack, so one
+ * quiet moment in a heavy scene cannot let the loop out. They may come from
+ * different frames, which pairs a worst with a least and errs toward staying
+ * put; on the frame that decides it they are the same frame. And the room
+ * left over has to be the same third of a frame that the branch below calls
+ * comfortable, so the scale that lands is one the scene has room in rather
+ * than one it can only just make.
+ */
+static int view_hd_step_up_fits(long long least_ahead,
+                                unsigned long long worst_picture)
+{
+    unsigned long long s = (unsigned long long)view_hd_s;
+    unsigned long long extra;
+
+    if (s < 1ull || least_ahead <= 0) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    extra = worst_picture * (2ull * s + 1ull) / (s * s);
+    return (unsigned long long)least_ahead >= extra + 5000000ull;
+}
+
+static void view_hd_adapt(long long ahead, unsigned long long picture_ns)
+{
+    static unsigned over, under;
+    /* The clean stretch's own worst case, which is what the step up is
+     * tested against; both go whenever the stretch does. */
+    static unsigned long long worst_picture;
+    static long long least_ahead;
+
+    if (view_hd_want <= 1 || !view_hd_dynamic()) {
+        return;
+    }
+    if (view_picture_is_the_bill(ahead, picture_ns)) {
+        /* Late, the picture is a real part of why, and it cost one. A frame
+         * that went over by less than that is the pacer carrying a little
+         * debt, not a scene the machine cannot draw, and lowering the
+         * scale for it would trade sharpness away for nothing. */
+        under = 0;
+        worst_picture = 0ull;
+        least_ahead = 0;
+        over += VIEW_HD_MISS_W;
+        if (over >= VIEW_HD_DROP_AT && view_hd_s > 1) {
+            view_hd_apply(view_hd_s - 1);
+            over = 0;
+            fprintf(stderr, "pc-pace: the 3D layer cannot hold this scene --"
+                            " internal resolution now %dx (asked %dx)\n",
+                    view_hd_s, view_hd_want);
+            fflush(stderr);
+        }
+    } else if (ahead > 5000000LL) {
+        /* A third of the frame to spare. */
+        if (over > 0u) over--;
+        if (picture_ns > worst_picture) worst_picture = picture_ns;
+        if (least_ahead == 0 || ahead < least_ahead) least_ahead = ahead;
+        if (++under >= VIEW_HD_CLEAR_N && view_hd_s < view_hd_want
+            && view_hd_step_up_fits(least_ahead, worst_picture)) {
+            view_hd_apply(view_hd_s + 1);
+            under = 0;
+            /* Measured at the scale just left behind, so worth nothing at
+             * the one just arrived at. */
+            worst_picture = 0ull;
+            least_ahead = 0;
+            fprintf(stderr, "pc-pace: room again, internal resolution back"
+                            " to %dx (asked %dx)\n",
+                    view_hd_s, view_hd_want);
+            fflush(stderr);
+        }
+    } else {
+        /* Neither late nor comfortable: it counts for the recovery, which
+         * wants a clean stretch, and against nothing. */
+        under = 0;
+        worst_picture = 0ull;
+        least_ahead = 0;
+    }
+}
